Found during k96 (worker). A source file's mode is not kept on install, for skills/ and .claude/ alike: karr's .claude/skills/karr-single-binary/scripts/benchmark.sh is 0775 in the source and installs as 0664, so a skill that tells the model to run scripts/x.sh gets a non-executable file. Expected: the executable bit of a source file survives render/apply (git and url sources and local); check/lock hashing decides whether a mode change counts as drift. Pre-existing, not a regression.

2026-09-27 00:33 Cause (worker): render.build reads only bytes (readFileSync), apply writes via atomicWrite with the default mode, and the url backend's tar reader ignores the header mode. Git caches and local sources do keep the bit on disk; it is lost between render and apply. Writing the regression test first (test/modes.test.ts).

2026-09-27 00:39 Fix in place, regression test test/modes.test.ts red before / green after (4 tests: local both layouts + .njk, source mode change, git + check, url). Full npm test pending: the host's loadguard refuses it under memory pressure; retrying.

2026-09-27 00:42 Changed: src/fsutil.ts (isExecutable = owner bit 0o100, the one git records; atomicWrite(path, data, executable) creates 0777/0666 less umask), src/render.ts (executables(item, sourceDir): install paths whose source file is executable, .njk passes its bit on), src/engine.ts (skills only: plan item gets executable set, skilletor's own skill .gitignore excluded), src/apply.ts (PlanItem.executable; a file is correct only if bytes AND bit match; bit-only rewrite is an update, never 'overwritten'), src/sources/url.ts (tar reader keeps the entry's owner exec bit when extracting; git caches and local sources already had it on disk), test/helpers/tar.ts (optional entry mode), test/modes.test.ts (new), dist/skilletor.js rebuilt, spec §5 + §6.2. DRIFT DECISION: the bit is not lock state and not a check drift kind. Lock hashes stay bytes-only; each sync compares the bit like the bytes (render-and-compare) and fixes it. Why: a source-side mode change always moves the source version (commit / new archive / local always re-rendered), so the existing source test already covers it; a local chmod is like a local edit, which check never sees either (it never reads installed files); folding mode into the hash would have made every file an earlier version installed read as locally overwritten on the first sync. Agents/rules: one Markdown file the harness reads, never runs - install without the bit, like every generated file (Codex TOML, rules file, skill .gitignore).

